course: dictionary
lesson: 11125
title: Wholly and partly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Ukraine is the largest country wholly in Europe.» Wholly means completely or fully. Ukraine lies all inside Europe, with no part of it in another continent.
explain: The clue «not wholly correct» means partly right: some parts are right and some are wrong. Wholly is like a dimmer switch turned all the way up. Partly is the middle, and not wholly is somewhere short of full.
explain: Try the swap test. Put completely in place of wholly. «Wholly owned» becomes «completely owned», which still makes sense, so wholly means completely. Look also for the phrase «wholly or partly», which means either completely or to some degree.
fact: «Wholly» means completely, fully or entirely.
fact: «Not wholly» means partly, but not completely.
fact: In maths and logic, «wholly» means every part, all 100%.
fact: «Wholly» is a formal word, often found in exams and official writing.
fact: Russia is bigger than Ukraine only if you count its Asian land, so Ukraine is the largest country wholly in Europe.
exercise: «Ukraine, with its capital Kyiv, is the largest country wholly in Europe.» What does wholly mean here? | Very small | Partly in Asia | Near the sea | *Completely inside Europe
exercise: «The plan was not wholly correct.» What does not wholly correct mean? | *Partly right, with some parts wrong | Completely right | Written down in a book | Totally wrong in every part
exercise: Mia says the company is «wholly owned» by her uncle. Which word could replace wholly? | Sometimes | Barely | Quietly | *Completely
test: «Germany, with its capital Berlin, has the most people of any country wholly in Europe.» What does wholly mean here? | Across two continents | Very quickly | Only just | *Entirely inside Europe
test: Ali says the answer is «wholly or partly right». What does this phrase mean? | Right in every way, with no doubt at all | Right only in the morning | *Either fully right or right to some degree | Wrong in every way
exam: Which word best replaces wholly in «wholly responsible»? | Quietly | *Completely | Barely | Sometimes
